    "title": "Kishore Papineni - ACL Anthology",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "![ACL Logo](https://aclanthology.org/images/acl-logo.svg) ACL Anthology ⟨1⟩\n  * About⟨2⟩\n    * Announcements⟨3⟩\n    * Communication channels⟨4⟩\n    * Related work⟨5⟩\n    * Copyright⟨6⟩\n    * * * *\n    * Credits⟨7⟩\n    * Volunteer⟨8⟩\n    * Development⟨9⟩\n    * Feedback⟨10⟩\n  * Using⟨2⟩\n    * Citing papers⟨11⟩\n    * Links in the Anthology⟨12⟩\n    * Data access⟨13⟩\n    * * * *\n    * All FAQs⟨14⟩\n    * * * *\n    * ###### Details\n    * Anthology identifiers⟨15⟩\n    * Names⟨16⟩\n    * ORCID iDs⟨17⟩\n    * DOIs⟨18⟩\n    * Verified authors⟨19⟩\n  * Contributions⟨2⟩\n    * Submissions⟨20⟩\n    * Corrections⟨21⟩\n    * Author pages⟨22⟩\n    * Attachments⟨23⟩\n  * GitHub⟨24⟩\n\n\n##  Kishore Papineni ⟨22⟩\n* * *\n#### 2020\npdf ⟨25⟩bib ⟨26⟩abs⟨27⟩ ⟨28⟩\n**Text Segmentation by Cross Segment Attention⟨29⟩**  \nMichal Lukasik⟨30⟩ | Boris Dadachev⟨31⟩ | Kishore Papineni⟨32⟩ | Gonçalo Simões⟨33⟩  \nProceedings of the 2020 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)⟨34⟩\nDocument and discourse segmentation are two fundamental NLP tasks pertaining to breaking up text into constituents, which are commonly used to help downstream tasks such as information retrieval or text summarization. In this work, we propose three transformer-based architectures and provide comprehensive comparisons with previously proposed approaches on three standard datasets. We establish a new state-of-the-art, reducing in particular the error rates by a large margin in all cases. We further analyze model sizes and find that we can build models with many fewer parameters while keeping good performance, thus facilitating real-world applications.\n#### 2008\npdf ⟨35⟩bib ⟨36⟩\n**Multiple Reorderings in Phrase-Based Machine Translation⟨37⟩**  \nNiyu Ge⟨38⟩ | Abe Ittycheriah⟨39⟩ | Kishore Papineni⟨32⟩  \nProceedings of the ACL-08: HLT Second Workshop on Syntax and Structure in Statistical Translation (SSST-2)⟨40⟩\n#### 2007\npdf ⟨41⟩bib ⟨42⟩\n**Hierarchical System Combination for Machine Translation⟨43⟩**  \nFei Huang⟨44⟩ | Kishore Papineni⟨32⟩  \nProceedings of the 2007 Joint Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing and Computational Natural Language Learning (EMNLP-CoNLL)⟨45⟩\n#### 2006\npdf ⟨46⟩bib ⟨47⟩\n**Distortion Models for Statistical Machine Translation⟨48⟩**  \nYaser Al-Onaizan⟨49⟩ | Kishore Papineni⟨32⟩  \nProceedings of the 21st International Conference on Computational Linguistics and 44th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics⟨50⟩\n#### 2005\npdf ⟨51⟩bib ⟨52⟩\n**Inner-Outer Bracket Models for Word Alignment using Hidden Blocks⟨53⟩**  \nBing Zhao⟨54⟩ | Niyu Ge⟨38⟩ | Kishore Papineni⟨32⟩  \nProceedings of Human Language Technology Conference and Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing⟨55⟩\n#### 2003\npdf ⟨56⟩bib ⟨57⟩\n**Language Model Based Arabic Word Segmentation⟨58⟩**  \nYoung-Suk Lee⟨59⟩ | Kishore Papineni⟨32⟩ | Salim Roukos⟨60⟩ | Ossama Emam⟨61⟩ | Hany Hassan⟨62⟩  \nProceedings of the 41st Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics⟨63⟩\npdf ⟨64⟩bib ⟨65⟩\n**TIPS : A Translingual Information Processing System⟨66⟩**  \nYaser Al-Onaizan⟨49⟩ | Radu Florian⟨67⟩ | Martin Franz⟨68⟩ | Hany Hassan⟨62⟩ | Young-Suk Lee⟨59⟩ | J. Scott McCarley⟨69⟩ | Kishore Papineni⟨32⟩ | Salim Roukos⟨60⟩ | Jeffrey Sorensen⟨70⟩ | Christoph Tillmann⟨71⟩ | Todd Ward⟨72⟩ | Fei Xia⟨73⟩  \nCompanion Volume of the Proceedings of HLT-NAACL 2003 - Demonstrations⟨74⟩\n#### 2002\npdf ⟨75⟩bib ⟨76⟩\n**B leu: a Method for Automatic Evaluation of Machine Translation⟨77⟩**  \nKishore Papineni⟨32⟩ | Salim Roukos⟨60⟩ | Todd Ward⟨72⟩ | Wei-Jing Zhu⟨78⟩  \nProceedings of the 40th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics⟨79⟩\n ⟨80⟩bib ⟨81⟩\n**Machine Translation Evaluation: N-grams to the Rescue⟨82⟩**  \nKishore Papineni⟨32⟩  \nProceedings of the Third International Conference on Language Resources and Evaluation (LREC’02)⟨83⟩\n#### 2001\npdf ⟨84⟩bib ⟨85⟩\n**Why Inverse Document Frequency?⟨86⟩**  \nKishore Papineni⟨32⟩  \nSecond Meeting of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics⟨87⟩\nSearch⟨88⟩\n##### Co-authors\n  * Salim Roukos⟨60⟩ 3\n  * Yaser Al-Onaizan⟨49⟩ 2\n  * Niyu Ge⟨38⟩ 2\n  * Hany Hassan Awadalla⟨62⟩ 2\n  * Young-Suk Lee⟨59⟩ 2\nshow all...\n  * Todd Ward⟨72⟩ 2\n  * Boris Dadachev⟨31⟩ 1\n  * Ossama Emam⟨61⟩ 1\n  * Radu Florian⟨67⟩ 1\n  * Martin Franz⟨68⟩ 1\n  * Fei Huang⟨44⟩ 1\n  * Abe Ittycheriah⟨39⟩ 1\n  * Michal Lukasik⟨30⟩ 1\n  * J. Scott McCarley⟨69⟩ 1\n  * Gonçalo Simões⟨33⟩ 1\n  * Jeffrey Sorensen⟨70⟩ 1\n  * Christoph Tillmann⟨71⟩ 1\n  * Fei Xia⟨73⟩ 1\n  * Bing Zhao⟨54⟩ 1\n  * Wei-Jing Zhu⟨78⟩ 1\n\n\n##### Venues\n  * ACL⟨89⟩3\n  * EMNLP⟨90⟩3\n  * NAACL⟨91⟩2\n  * COLING⟨92⟩1\n  * CoNLL⟨93⟩1\nshow all...\n  * HLT⟨94⟩1\n  * LREC⟨95⟩1\n  * SSST⟨96⟩1\n\n\n Fix author⟨97⟩\n![Creative Commons License](https://i.creativecommons.org/l/by/4.0/88x31.png)⟨98⟩ ACL materials are Copyright © 1963–2026 ACL; other materials are copyrighted by their respective copyright holders. Materials prior to 2016 here are licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 3.0 International License⟨99⟩. Permission is granted to make copies for the purposes of teaching and research. Materials published in or after 2016 are licensed on a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License⟨100⟩.\nThe ACL Anthology is managed and built by the ACL Anthology team⟨7⟩ of volunteers.\n_Site last built on 22 July 2026 at 14:46 UTC withcommit 280e4ed⟨101⟩._\n",
